# Authentication and authorization (Cognito and other OIDC providers)

The platform already validates tokens for you. You do **not** write token-checking code. You fill in settings and choose which endpoints need which permission.

Code lives in `Infrastructure/Extensions/AuthenticationExtensions.cs`, `Infrastructure/Extensions/AuthorizationPolicyExtensions.cs` and `Auth/CognitoTokenValidator.cs`. Do not edit them. If something is missing, raise a platform ticket.
{{- if model.manifest.cognito != null }}

## This service is already set up for Cognito

The generator filled in the Cognito settings in `appsettings.json`:

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| `Forge:Authentication:Scheme` | `{{ model.manifest.cognito.scheme }}` |
| `Forge:Oidc:Provider` | `Cognito` |
| `Forge:Oidc:Authority` | `{{ model.manifest.cognito.authority }}` |
| `Forge:Oidc:ClientIds` | {{ for client_id in model.manifest.cognito.client_ids }}`{{ client_id }}`{{ if !for.last }}, {{ end }}{{ end }} |
| `Forge:Authorization:AdminGroup` | `{{ model.manifest.cognito.admin_group }}` |
{{- if model.manifest.cognito.allow_api_key }}

The scheme is `Both`: a request with a bearer token is checked as a Cognito login, and a request without one is checked as an API key. Set `Forge:Authentication:ApiKey` (or use Secrets Manager) before API key callers can get in. API key callers have no groups, so they cannot use `AdminOnly` endpoints.
{{- end }}

You only need to add a permission to each endpoint. To use other values in another environment, override them in `appsettings.<Environment>.json` or with environment variables. The sections below explain every setting.
{{- end }}

## Modes

Set `Forge:Authentication:Scheme`:

| Value | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `ApiKey` (default) | Callers send an `X-Api-Key` header. |
| `Bearer` | Callers send `Authorization: Bearer <access token>`. |
| `Both` | A bearer token is used when present, otherwise the API key. |

Any other value (including a typo such as `bearer`) stops the service at startup.

## Setting up Cognito

1. Create the user pool, the app clients and the groups in AWS. The platform does not create them.
2. Fill in the settings (all values are IDs, not secrets, and no client secret is needed):

```json
{
  "Forge": {
    "Authentication": { "Scheme": "Bearer" },
    "Oidc": {
      "Provider": "Cognito",
      "Authority": "https://cognito-idp.<region>.amazonaws.com/<user-pool-id>",
      "ClientIds": [ "<web-app-client-id>", "<mobile-app-client-id>" ]
    },
    "Authorization": { "AdminGroup": "Admin" }
  }
}
```

| Setting | What to put there |
|---|---|
| `Authority` | `https://cognito-idp.<region>.amazonaws.com/<user-pool-id>` |
| `ClientIds` | The app client ID of each frontend that calls this service. Tokens issued to any other app client are rejected. |
| `AdminGroup` | The Cognito group allowed to use `AdminOnly` endpoints. Default: `Admin`. |

Use `appsettings.<Environment>.json` or environment variables for per-environment values. Environment variable names use double underscores, for example `Forge__Oidc__Authority` and `Forge__Oidc__ClientIds__0`.

Teams usually share one user pool per vertical, with one app client per frontend.

### What is checked on every request

- Signature, issuer and expiry (always).
- `token_use` must be `access`. ID tokens are rejected.
- `client_id` must be one of `ClientIds`.

Cognito access tokens have no normal `aud` claim, so `Audience` is not used for Cognito. Older services stored the client ID in `Audience`. That still works when `ClientIds` is empty, but it is deprecated. Move the value to `ClientIds`.

Claims keep their original Cognito names (`username`, `cognito:groups`, `client_id`). The user name is the `username` claim.

## Protecting endpoints

Every endpoint already requires a signed-in caller. Add a policy when it needs more.

Built in: `AdminOnly`, which needs the group set in `AdminGroup`.

```csharp
[HttpDelete("{id}")]
[Authorize(Policy = "AdminOnly")]
public async Task<IActionResult> DeleteAsync(string id, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // ...
}
```

Your own policies are defined in settings. You choose the names:

```json
{
  "Forge": {
    "Authorization": {
      "Policies": {
        "BillingAccess": { "Groups": [ "Billing", "Finance" ] },
        "ReportsRead": { "Scopes": [ "reports/read" ] },
        "BillingAdmin": { "Groups": [ "Billing" ], "Scopes": [ "billing/write" ] }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

```csharp
[Authorize(Policy = "BillingAccess")]
```

Rules:
- The user needs **any one** of the listed groups, and **any one** of the listed scopes.
- If both lists are filled in, the user needs both.
- A policy needs at least one group or scope.
- `AdminOnly` is reserved.
- Mistakes in these settings stop the service at startup.

Groups come from the `cognito:groups` claim for Cognito and from `groups` for other providers. Change it with `Forge:Authorization:GroupClaimType` if your provider uses another claim name.

Scopes come from the space-separated `scope` claim (or `scp`). Custom scopes in Cognito, such as `reports/read`, come from a resource server defined in your user pool.

API key callers have no groups or scopes. They cannot satisfy `AdminOnly` or your own policies, even in `Both` mode.

## What this does not cover

- **Frontend sign-in, callback, refresh and logout** are separate. The frontend must send the **access token**, not the ID token.
- **Sign-out does not revoke a token.** An access token stays valid until it expires.
- **Creating the AWS resources** (pool, app clients, groups) is done in AWS or your infrastructure code.

## Tests

`tests/{{ model.names.test_project_name }}/CognitoAuthenticationTests.cs` covers the platform rules with real signed tokens, so nothing calls AWS. Keep it. Add your own tests for your endpoints in separate files.
